  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "29cefde8",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "**What is this dataset?**\n",
    "\n",
    "This data comes from a real online course (a MOOC — \"Massive Open Online Course\") and tracks what\n",
    "7,047 students did while taking it. Every time a student clicked, watched, submitted, or interacted\n",
    "with anything in the course, that single moment got logged as one row of data. Think of it like a\n",
    "security-camera log for a classroom, except instead of \"person walked past door at 9:03am,\" it's\n",
    "\"student #482 did something with course item #7 at second 26,391 of the course.\"\n",
    "\n",
    "There are three files, and they all link together through a shared `actionid`:\n",
    "- **`mooc_actions.tsv`** — the core log: *who* (`userid`) did *what* (`targetid`, meaning which\n",
    "  video/quiz/forum post/etc.) and *when* (`timestamp`, in seconds since the course started).\n",
    "- **`mooc_action_features.tsv`** — four extra numeric measurements about each action (the exact\n",
    "  meaning isn't published by the dataset creators, but they behave like engineered behavioral\n",
    "  signals, e.g. related to timing or intensity of the action).\n",
    "- **`mooc_action_labels.tsv`** — a simple yes/no flag: was this the student's *last* action before\n",
    "  they disappeared from the course and never came back (`label = 1`), or did they keep going\n",
    "  (`label = 0`)? This is the \"drop-out\" signal we'll use later.\n",
    "\n",
    "**What does the output above tell us?**\n",
    "`(411749, 4) (411749, 2) (411749, 5)` means we have **411,749 total actions** logged across all\n",
    "students, and each of the three files has the expected number of columns. The preview table shows\n",
    "student `0` doing three different things (`targetid` 0, 1, 2) in the first 41 seconds of the course\n",
    "— a normal, active start."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "1cfcb4fe",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### 3. Take a Manageable Subset and Build the Graph\n",
    "The full dataset has over 400,000 actions — far too many to draw or even compute on quickly for a class exercise. So we sort all actions by time and keep just the **first 500 actions** (the very beginning of the course). We then turn this into a graph: every **student** and every **course activity** becomes a node, and we draw an edge between a student and an activity every time that student interacted with it (repeat interactions just make the edge \"heavier\" via a `weight`)."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 3,
   "id": "ae66fcb0",
   "metadata": {
    "execution": {
     "iopub.execute_input": "2026-09-20T03:40:11.557883Z",
     "iopub.status.busy": "2026-09-20T03:40:11.557150Z",
     "iopub.status.idle": "2026-09-20T03:40:11.575528Z",
     "shell.execute_reply": "2026-09-20T03:40:11.574806Z"
    }
   },
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "Users: 78, Targets: 20\n",
      "98 nodes, 383 edges\n"
     ]
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "action_data_sorted = action_data.sort_values(\"timestamp\")\n",
    "subset = action_data_sorted.head(500).copy()\n",
    "subset.merge(action_features, on="actionid").merge(action_labels, on="actionid").to_csv("mooc_subset_500.csv", index=False)\n",
    "# 1. clean subset FIRST\n",
    "subset = subset.dropna(subset=[\"userid\", \"targetid\"])\n",
    "subset[\"userid\"] = subset[\"userid\"].astype(int)\n",
    "subset[\"targetid\"] = subset[\"targetid\"].astype(int)\n",
    "\n",
    "# 2. THEN derive users/targets from the now-clean subset (same cell, same run)\n",
    "users = subset.userid.unique()\n",
    "targets = subset.targetid.unique()\n",
    "print(f\"Users: {len(users)}, Targets: {len(targets)}\")\n",
    "# 3. build the graph \n",
    "G = nx.Graph()\n",
    "G.add_nodes_from([f\"u_{u}\" for u in users], node_type=\"user\")\n",
    "G.add_nodes_from([f\"t_{t}\" for t in targets], node_type=\"target\")\n",
    "\n",
    "for row in subset.itertuples(index=False):\n",
    "    u, t = f\"u_{row.userid}\", f\"t_{row.targetid}\"\n",
    "    if G.has_edge(u, t):\n",
    "        G[u][t][\"weight\"] += 1\n",
    "    else:\n",
    "        G.add_edge(u, t, weight=1)\n",
    "\n",
    "print(f\"{G.number_of_nodes()} nodes, {G.number_of_edges()} edges\")\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "ea606ba6",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "**What does this result mean?**\n",
    "\n",
    "Out of the first 500 actions, **78 different students** interacted with **20 different course\n",
    "activities**. Our graph has **98 nodes** total (78 students + 20 activities) and **383 edges**\n",
    "(connections between a student and an activity they touched).\n",
    "\n",
    "This is a **bipartite graph**: a graph with two distinct groups of nodes (students and activities)\n",
    "where connections only ever go *between* the groups, never *within* a group. Students never connect\n",
    "directly to other students in this graph; they're only ever linked to each other indirectly, by both\n",
    "having interacted with the same activity. That indirect linking is exactly what will let us compute\n",
    "things like \"diameter\" in the next step."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "7d5a8751",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### 4. Check Connectivity and Measure the Diameter\n",
    "Before measuring distances in a graph, we need to know if it's fully connected — meaning can you get from any node to any other node by following edges, or are there separate \"islands\"? If it's connected, we can then measure the **diameter**: the longest of all the shortest paths between any two nodes. It tells us the maximum number of \"hops\" you'd ever need to get from one node to the farthest possible node in the graph. We compute it two ways — once using NetworkX's built-in function, and once by coding the underlying algorithm (breadth-first search, or BFS) by hand, to show it isn't a black box."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 4,
   "id": "d81ea082",
   "metadata": {
    "execution": {
     "iopub.execute_input": "2026-09-20T03:40:11.577340Z",
     "iopub.status.busy": "2026-09-20T03:40:11.576827Z",
     "iopub.status.idle": "2026-09-20T03:40:11.592553Z",
     "shell.execute_reply": "2026-09-20T03:40:11.591974Z"
    }
   },
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "Is connected: True\n",
      "Number of connected components: 1\n",
      "Largest component: 98 of 98 nodes\n",
      "Diameter (networkx): 5\n",
      "Diameter (hand-coded BFS): 5\n"
     ]
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "# 1. Connectivity overview (on full G)\n",
    "print(\"Is connected:\", nx.is_connected(G))\n",
    "print(\"Number of connected components:\", nx.number_connected_components(G))\n",
    "\n",
    "# 2. Find the largest connected component \n",
    "components = list(nx.connected_components(G))\n",
    "largest_cc = max(components, key=len)\n",
    "print(f\"Largest component: {len(largest_cc)} of {G.number_of_nodes()} nodes\")\n",
    "\n",
    "# 3. Build the subgraph from it \n",
    "G_lcc = G.subgraph(largest_cc).copy()\n",
    "degrees = dict(G_lcc.degree())\n",
    "\n",
    "# 4. Diameter — library method\n",
    "diameter_nx = nx.diameter(G_lcc)\n",
    "print(\"Diameter (networkx):\", diameter_nx)\n",
    "\n",
    "# 5. Diameter — hand-coded BFS, for intuition\n",
    "def bfs_eccentricity(graph, source):\n",
    "    visited = {source: 0}      # {node: distance from source}. Start node is 0 steps from itself.\n",
    "    frontier = [source]        # the \"current ring\" of nodes we're exploring outward from\n",
    "    while frontier:             # keep going as long as there's a ring left to expand\n",
    "        next_frontier = []\n",
    "        for node in frontier:                    # look at every node in the current ring\n",
    "            for neighbor in graph.neighbors(node):    # check all of its direct connections\n",
    "                if neighbor not in visited:            # if we haven't reached this node yet\n",
    "                    visited[neighbor] = visited[node] + 1   # it's one step farther than \"node\" was\n",
    "                    next_frontier.append(neighbor)          # add it to the next ring to explore\n",
    "        frontier = next_frontier    # move outward to the next ring\n",
    "    return max(visited.values())    # the farthest distance we ever recorded = this node's \"eccentricity\"\n",
    "\n",
    "def graph_diameter(graph):\n",
    "    return max(bfs_eccentricity(graph, node) for node in graph.nodes())\n",
    "\n",
    "diameter_manual = graph_diameter(G_lcc)\n",
    "print(\"Diameter (hand-coded BFS):\", diameter_manual)\n",
    "assert diameter_nx == diameter_manual\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "6bc3e0c0",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "**What does this result mean?**\n",
    "\n",
    "`Is connected: True` with only **1 connected component** means every single one of our 98 nodes can\n",
    "reach every other node by following some path of edges — there are no isolated \"islands\" of students\n",
    "who are cut off from the rest. That makes sense here: with only 20 activities shared among 78\n",
    "students, most students end up linked to each other indirectly through common activities early on.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Diameter = 5**, confirmed by both methods (library function and our own hand-written BFS search).\n",
    "In plain terms: no matter which two nodes you pick in this graph, even the two that are \"farthest\n",
    "apart\" you can always get from one to the other in **5 hops or fewer**, by alternating between students and activities (student → activity → student → activity...). A small diameter like this\n",
    "is typical of bipartite graphs where there are far fewer \"hub\" nodes (20 activities) than \"leaf\"\n",
    "nodes (78 students) the small number of shared activities acts like a shortcut that keeps everyone\n",
    "close together in the network."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "616546c6",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### 5. A Second Metric: Average Degree, Clustering, and Drop-out Comparison\n",
    "Diameter tells us about the *shape* of the whole graph. Now we'll look at individual nodes using **degree** — how many connections a node has — and use it to ask a real question about the data: do students who eventually drop out of the course behave differently (in terms of how many activities they touch) than students who don't?"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "e700f017",
   "metadata": {
    "execution": {
     "iopub.execute_input": "2026-09-20T03:40:11.594021Z",
     "iopub.status.busy": "2026-09-20T03:40:11.593610Z",
     "iopub.status.idle": "2026-09-20T03:40:11.605324Z",
     "shell.execute_reply": "2026-09-20T03:40:11.604611Z"
    }
   },
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "Average degree: 7.82\n",
      "Average clustering coefficient: 0.0000  (0 expected — bipartite, no triangles)\n",
      "                 mean  count\n",
      "dropped_out                 \n",
      "False        4.878378     74\n",
      "True         5.500000      4\n"
     ]
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "# average number of connections per node\n",
    "degrees = dict(G_lcc.degree())\n",
    "print(f\"Average degree: {sum(degrees.values())/len(degrees):.2f}\")\n",
    "print(f\"Average clustering coefficient: {nx.average_clustering(G_lcc):.4f}  (0 expected — bipartite, no triangles)\")\n",
    "\n",
    "# Connecting degree to drop-out labels\n",
    "subset_labeled = subset.merge(action_labels, on=\"actionid\", how=\"left\") \n",
    "dropout_userids = set(subset_labeled.loc[subset_labeled.label == 1, \"userid\"]) # This adds a new column that's simply True if that student's ID is in our dropout_userids set, False otherwise.\n",
    "\n",
    "user_degree_df = pd.DataFrame([\n",
    "    {\"userid\": int(float(n[2:])), \"degree\": d} for n, d in degrees.items() if n.startswith(\"u_\")\n",
    "]) \n",
    "\n",
    "# This splits all students into two groups — dropped out vs. didn't drop out — and for each group, calculates the average degree (mean) and how many students are in that group (count).\n",
    "user_degree_df[\"dropped_out\"] = user_degree_df.userid.isin(dropout_userids)\n",
    "print(user_degree_df.groupby(\"dropped_out\")[\"degree\"].agg([\"mean\", \"count\"]))\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "4c047b70",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "**What does this result mean?**\n",
    "\n",
    "- **Average degree = 7.82** — on average, each node (student or activity) in this graph is\n",
    "  connected to about 8 other nodes. Since students and activities have very different roles, this\n",
    "  average blends two very different numbers: most individual students only touch a handful of\n",
    "  activities, while a few popular activities are touched by dozens of students (we'll see this\n",
    "  clearly in the extra chart below).\n",
    "\n",
    "- **Average clustering coefficient = 0.0000** — this is expected, not an error. Clustering measures\n",
    "  how often a node's neighbors are also connected to *each other* (forming a triangle). But in a\n",
    "  bipartite graph, students never connect to other students directly, and activities never connect\n",
    "  to other activities directly — so triangles are mathematically impossible here. A clustering score\n",
    "  of exactly 0 is simply confirming the graph's structure is doing what we expect.\n",
    "  \n",
    "- **Drop-out comparison** — students who eventually dropped out had a slightly *higher* average\n",
    "  degree (**5.50**) than students who didn't (**4.88**). However, this needs a big caveat: only\n",
    "  **4 students** in this small 500-action subset actually dropped out, versus 74 who didn't. A\n",
    "  difference based on just 4 people is not something we can treat as a reliable pattern — it could\n",
    "  easily be one or two atypical students skewing the average. We'd need a much larger sample (or the\n",
    "  full dataset) before treating this as a real finding rather than a coincidence."
   ]
  },
