   "source": [
    "**How to read these plots:**\n",
    "\n",
    "**Left (histogram):** Most nodes pile up on the low end of the degree scale — many students and\n",
    "several activities only have a handful of connections. But the tail stretches out much farther,\n",
    "because a small number of activities have very high degree (one activity alone is connected to\n",
    "almost every student in the subset). This \"many nodes with few links, a few nodes with tons of\n",
    "links\" shape is called a **skewed** or **long-tailed** distribution, and it's extremely common in\n",
    "real-world networks — it's the same basic pattern behind why a handful of websites get most of the\n",
    "internet's traffic while millions of others get very little.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Right (bar chart):** This zooms in on exactly which activities are the popular ones. Activity\n",
    "**`t_1`** is connected to **74 out of 78 students** — essentially everyone touched it, strongly\n",
    "suggesting it's a required first step in the course (like an intro video, sign-up form, or welcome\n",
    "page). Activity `t_3` is the second most common, reaching 54 students, and the rest drop off more\n",
    "gradually. Together, these two charts explain *why* our diameter stayed small: with hub activities\n",
    "like `t_1` connecting to almost every student, any two students are likely just 2 hops apart (through\n",
    "that shared activity), which keeps the whole graph's diameter low even as more students join."
   ]
  }
